#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171774 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "231de5e4-8c55-5129-baaf-57673050ddbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8505" :question/problem "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171775 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0444de80-19f1-5251-b159-5dc55756bb19" :question/math-academy-id "e-8506" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171776 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8576" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed78d2ea-f4b9-5e3c-b5af-be56dbec8314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/7470460ab6f2d7e0bfde9585c6447a75abbea498d9c0628c23d6b69bc9738c46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324470} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb515b1-5173-5458-8be2-49be2435908d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324471} {:answer/id #uuid "a7872c33-3488-509d-a696-6fc905fe7388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/d8a84684bd0ea9b96e9e3901b4dfec5e5568a80ad44202161a76414ce0a4edfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324472} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc4a9a3-060b-5cdd-9489-ba14d8ec6d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/f5ee96c6ad4540cde69b78af9a13e4513a24f6c537f69c4bee5ec0d440da56c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324473} {:answer/id #uuid "38dabdd0-12c5-5dc8-936b-38dfecb323fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/questi